d tag; the newer redistribution model does not always expose a one-for-one equivalent for every legacy path-type/area condition.</p><h4>Why a clean commit can still be wrong</h4><p>A configuration can migrate without syntax errors but broaden or narrow the candidate set. A rule that intended “only these E1 routes from this area” can become a more generic prefix/tag policy unless the original intent is explicitly reconstructed.</p><h4>Pre-migration inventory</h4><p>Build a route-policy matrix before the change: source protocol, area, path type, prefix, tag, expected redistribution result, expected BGP advertisement, and expected winning RIB route. Include routes that must remain denied.</p><h4>Validation</h4><p>After migration, inspect the source OSPF RIB/LSDB, redistribution match result, BGP RIB, and peer advertisement. Test intra-area, inter-area, E1, E2, NSSA, and tagged routes where the production policy differentiates them.</p><h4>Design lesson</h4><p>Tags can become a durable cross-version/cross-vendor provenance marker when vendor-specific path-type matching differs, but only if the tag is set consistently and verified after conversion.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>Inspect legacy OSPF Export Rules
Inspect Advanced Routing Engine Redistribution Profile
Compare runtime route tables before/after migration</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the smallest reproducible lab for lesson 8, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">Palo Alto Networks</span><h3>9. Redistribution versus BGP export</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://docs.paloaltonetworks.com/ngfw/networking/configure-route-redistribution">https://docs.paloaltonetworks.com/ngfw/networking/configure-route-redistribution</a></p><h4>Two distinct questions</h4><p>Redistribution asks: “Should this route enter another routing protocol?” BGP export asks: “Should this BGP route be advertised to this peer or peer group?” Those are different policy boundaries and should not be collapsed into one mental model.</p><h4>Route lifecycle</h4><p>A connected/static/OSPF route first exists in the source routing table, then matches a redistribution profile or route map, then appears in the local BGP RIB, then may be filtered or modified by peer export policy. Each stage can independently permit, deny, or alter the route.</p><h4>Failure examples</h4><p>If the route is missing from the local BGP RIB, troubleshoot redistribution. If it is present locally but absent from one neighbor, troubleshoot export. If it is present at the peer but not selected, troubleshoot the peer’s own best-path process and next-hop reachability.</p><h4>Operational safety</h4><p>Use the narrowest policy stage that satisfies the requirement. A peer-specific exception should usually not be implemented by globally changing redistribution because that can affect every BGP peer.</p><h4>Interoperability</h4><p>This lifecycle maps cleanly to Cisco/Arista/FRR route-maps and FortiGate redistribution plus neighbor policy. Normalize the stage first, then syntax.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>Runtime routing table
Redistribution Profile / BGP Redist Rules
BGP peer/group Import/Export policy</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the smallest reproducible lab for lesson 9, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">Arista Networks</span><h3>10. EVPN Type-5 DCI re-origination</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://www.arista.com/en/um-eos/eos-configuring-evpn">https://www.arista.com/en/um-eos/eos-configuring-evpn</a></p><h4>Why a DCI gateway is a redistribution boundary</h4><p>Arista’s EVPN Type-5 multi-domain design allows a gateway to terminate one EVPN domain and re-originate IP-prefix reachability into another. The route is no longer merely reflected; it is represented in a new domain context with different RD/RT policy.</p><h4>Route identity and policy</h4><p>RDs keep otherwise-identical VPN routes unique, while RT import/export controls which VRFs accept them. At a DCI boundary, the gateway must map the source domain’s route into the destination domain without leaking internal topology or allowing the route to circulate back indefinitely.</p><h4>Loop prevention</h4><p>Re-origination creates a problem analogous to redistribution loops. Arista documents domain/path mechanisms such as D-path handling so a route that already traversed a domain boundary can be identified and rejected if it returns through another gateway.</p><h4>Troubleshooting</h4><p>Inspect the Type-5 route on both sides of the gateway: RD, RT, next hop, domain/path metadata, VRF import, and recursive next-hop resolution. A route can be valid in one domain and absent in another because of intentional RT policy.</p><h4>Design guidance</h4><p>Treat DCI gateways as policy boundaries. Document which prefixes cross, how they are summarized, what provenance metadata is carried, and how alternate gateways prevent re-entry.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>show bgp evpn
show bgp evpn route-type ip-prefix
show ip route vrf &lt;vrf&gt;</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the smallest reproducible lab for lesson 10, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">Arista Networks</span><h3>11. Single-gateway centralized routing and BUM</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://www.arista.com/en/um-eos/eos-evpn-vxlan-single-gateway-centralized-routing">https://www.arista.com/en/um-eos/eos-evpn-vxlan-single-gateway-centralized-routing</a></p><h4>Traditional centralized-gateway model</h4><p>In centralized routing, a small set of L3 VTEPs performs inter-subnet routing for the fabric. Redundant gateway designs can advertise both physical and virtual VTEP identities so endpoint gateway state remains stable during node failure.</p><h4>Why BUM can be duplicated</h4><p>If a topology really has only one centralized gateway or one MLAG pair, advertising multiple equivalent VTEP identities into the EVPN floodset can cause the same broadcast/unknown-unicast/multicast traffic to be replicated unnecessarily.</p><h4>Control plane can be correct but inefficient</h4><p>Type-3/IMET membership can be completely valid while the data plane sends more copies than required. This is not reachability failure; it is a scaling and bandwidth-efficiency problem.</p><h4>Troubleshooting</h4><p>When BUM traffic is unexpectedly high, inspect flood-list membership, VTEP identities, gateway mode, and whether the design actually needs both virtual and physical replication targets. Compare packet counters rather than relying only on BGP route counts.</p><h4>Design lesson</h4><p>BUM behavior is one reason to minimize unnecessary Layer-2 extension. A working EVPN control plane does not guarantee an efficient replication topology.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>show bgp evpn
show interfaces vxlan 1
Inspect IMET/flood-list membership and counters</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the smallest reproducible lab for lesson 11, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">Arista Networks</span><h3>12. Control plane versus data plane</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://www.arista.com/en/um-eos/eos-data-transfer">https://www.arista.com/en/um-eos/eos-data-transfer</a></p><h4>EOS forwarding model</h4><p>Arista explicitly separates routing control-plane computation from hardware Layer-3 switching. Protocols populate routing state; the system then programs forwarding information into hardware-optimized data structures.</p><h4>Why this matters in troubleshooting</h4><p>A route in BGP or even the RIB does not by itself prove the ASIC has a complete, usable adjacency. Next-hop recursion, interface state, ARP/ND, MTU, ACL/policy, or hardware programming can still prevent successful forwarding.</p><h4>Evidence chain</h4><p>Verify protocol adjacency and candidate route, then active RIB selection, next-hop resolution, forwarding adjacency, interface/error counters, and packet observation. This sequence prevents repeated control-plane tuning after the route-selection problem has already been solved.</p><h4>Redistribution connection</h4><p>Only routes represented in the local routing system become candidates for redistribution according to configured policy. Route origin, active status, and preference can therefore change which routes leave the box.</p><h4>Universal lesson</h4><p>RIB/FIB separation is one of the most portable multivendor troubleshooting concepts. It lets engineers compare behavior without getting trapped by vendor-specific CLI vocabulary.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>show ip route
show interfaces counters errors
Inspect forwarding/adjacency state</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the smallest reproducible lab for lesson 12, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">Aruba/HPE</span><h3>13. VSX logical VTEP with independent BGP speakers</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://www.arubanetworks.com/techdocs/AOS-CX/10.15/PDF/vsx.pdf">https://www.arubanetworks.com/techdocs/AOS-CX/10.15/PDF/vsx.pdf</a></p><h4>VSX dual identity</h4><p>AOS-CX VSX peers can be independent BGP speakers toward the fabric while presenting a common logical VTEP address for VXLAN forwarding. Separate control-plane addresses establish peerings; the shared VTEP identity gives remote devices one stable overlay next hop.</p><h4>Failure implication</h4><p>One peer’s BGP uplink can fail while the shared VTEP remains reachable through the surviving peer. Conversely, both BGP sessions can be healthy while host-facing VSX state, ISL behavior, or the logical VTEP data path is impaired.</p><h4>Underlay dependency</h4><p>Remote VTEPs still need an underlay route to the common VTEP address. EVPN does not create reachability to its own next hop. If the logical VTEP address is not recursively re